course: everyday
lesson: 1
title: Going up and down stairs safely
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Stairs are one of the places where people at home and at school fall most often, so it pays to use them carefully.
explain: Hold the handrail, take one step at a time and put your whole foot on each step; the rail gives you something to grab if you trip.
explain: Never run, push or play on stairs: going fast means a missed step turns into a tumble down many steps.
explain: When you carry something, carry it so you can still see your feet and the steps, and keep one hand free for the rail; make two small trips instead of one big one.
explain: For example, a boy in Denver carrying a tall laundry basket downstairs cannot see the steps, so he takes half the clothes first and holds the rail with his free hand.
explain: A boy in Ohio ran down the stairs in his socks carrying a laundry basket he could not see over; he slipped on the third step from the top, fell to the bottom and broke his arm.
explain: A little girl's family left a toy car on the stairs; her grandad stepped on it in the dark, fell and hurt his hip so badly he spent weeks in hospital.
fact: Falls on stairs are one of the most common ways people get hurt at home.
fact: Holding the handrail lets you catch yourself if you trip or slip.
fact: Going down stairs is riskier than going up, because a slip sends you forward and down.
fact: You should be able to see the steps and your feet when you carry something on the stairs.
fact: Toys, shoes and bags left on stairs are easy to trip over.
fact: Socks on smooth wooden stairs can slip; shoes or bare feet grip better.
fact: Running, pushing or playing games on stairs is dangerous.
fact: Stairs should be well lit, so you can see the edge of each step.
fact: Babies and toddlers need a safety gate at the top and bottom of the stairs.
fact: Looking at a phone while walking on stairs makes you miss steps.
fact: Falls are the most common reason children are taken to hospital with an injury.
fact: Older people who fall on stairs can break a hip, which can take months to heal.
fact: If someone falls and a bone may be broken (it hurts a lot or looks a strange shape), keep them still and get an adult at once; never try to pull it straight yourself.
exercise: What should you hold when you go down the stairs? | The handrail | The wall beside you | Your bag strap | Nothing at all
exercise: Why should you hold the handrail on stairs? | So you can catch yourself if you trip | Because it makes you go faster | Because the rail keeps the stairs clean | So the stairs do not wobble
exercise: How should you walk down stairs? | One step at a time | Two steps at a time | Jumping the last few steps | Sliding on the rail
exercise: Why is running on stairs dangerous? | A missed step can send you tumbling down | Running makes the stairs weaker and they may crack | It wears out your shoes too fast | It is only dangerous for adults
exercise: Which is more dangerous, going up or going down stairs? | Going down | Going up, because it is harder work | They are exactly the same | Neither is ever dangerous
exercise: How should you carry a big box down the stairs? | So you can still see your feet and the steps | High up in front of your face, held with both hands | On your head with both hands | Above your head with straight arms
exercise: Your arms are too full to hold the rail. What is wise? | Make two trips with smaller loads | Go down faster to get it over with | Close your eyes and count the steps | Slide down the stairs sitting on the box
exercise: What should you do with toys left on the stairs? | Pick them up and put them away | Step over them quickly in the dark | Leave them for someone else | Kick them down to the bottom
exercise: What is the safest thing on your feet on smooth wooden stairs? | Shoes with grip or bare feet | Thick, slippery socks pulled up high | Roller skates | Shoes with untied laces
exercise: Why should stairs be well lit? | So you can see the edge of each step | So the stairs do not get cold in the winter | So people can read on them | So the steps look bigger
exercise: Why do homes with toddlers have gates on the stairs? | To stop the toddler falling down | To keep the cat off the stairs at night | To keep the stairs warm | So adults go up more slowly
exercise: What is wrong with looking at a phone while you walk downstairs? | You may miss a step and fall | The phone works worse on stairs | It makes the stairs more slippery | Nothing, it is perfectly safe
exercise: Your friend wants to play tag on the school stairs. What is the best answer? | Let's play outside where it is safe | Yes, the stairs make the game much more fun | Only if we jump the bottom steps | Only if nobody is watching
test: A girl in Toronto carries a stack of books so tall she cannot see the steps. What should she do? | Carry fewer books so she can see | Go down faster before she drops them all | Walk down backwards | Lean back and keep going
test: Which habit keeps you safest on stairs? | Holding the rail and taking each step | Taking big leaps to get to the bottom sooner | Sliding down the banister | Running so you spend less time there
test: Tom slips on the top step of a long staircase. What helps him most? | His hand already on the rail | His heavy backpack pulling him back | Having his hands in his pockets | Being in a big hurry to get down
test: Why is a school bag left on the stairs a problem? | Someone can trip over it and fall | It makes the stairs dirty and muddy for everyone | It blocks the light | It makes the stairs weaker
test: Sam is in his socks on shiny wooden stairs. What would make him safer? | Taking the socks off or wearing shoes | Running down quickly | Holding a cup of juice | Pulling his socks up high over his ankles
test: The light on the stairs is broken at night. What is the safest choice? | Turn on another light and hold the rail | Go down quickly in the dark | Jump the steps you cannot see | Carry a big box in front of you to feel the way
test: What would happen if two children pushed each other on the stairs? | One could fall down many steps | Nothing, because stairs are soft and safe | They would only ever slip one step | It would just make the game faster
test: Which is true about stairs? | Many falls at home happen on stairs | Only old people ever fall on stairs | Stairs are safest when you run | Handrails are only for decoration
test: A mum carries a baby upstairs. Why is her other hand on the rail? | So she can hold on if she slips | Because the baby likes the rail | To keep the rail clean | To count the steps
test: carrying the laundry basket down, cant see the steps lol, just go for it? | No, take half now so you can see the steps | Yes, just go a bit faster | Yes, if you shut your eyes and count every step | Yes, if you lean way back
test: mum!! lego all over the stairs again | Pick it up before someone trips on it | Leave it, people can step over it | Kick it down to the bottom step | Only matters at night
test: in my socks, wood stairs, kinda slippy | Take the socks off or put shoes on | Pull the socks up higher | Just run so you don't slip | Hold your phone out in front for balance
test: texting my mate on the way downstairs, its fine innit | No, you can miss a step and fall | Yes, phones help you balance on stairs | Yes, if you go quickly | Only a problem going up
test: bro just slid down the stairs on a sleeping bag, he's crying and his arm looks weird | Keep him still and get an adult straight away | Tell him to shake it off and keep playing | Pull his arm straight to fix it | Give him a drink and send him to bed
test: why's mum so mad about my lego on the stairs lol | Someone can step on it, fall and get badly hurt | Lego gets lost when it is on the stairs | Stairs get scratched by the hard plastic bricks for good | It makes the stairs harder to clean
test: nan's got the shopping and the stairs light's broken. what do i do | Carry a bag for her and turn on a lamp or torch | Run past her so you are out of the way | Leave it, she has done it for years | Tell her to hurry before it gets darker
test: did u hear, Jack's grandad fell down the stairs over a skateboard, broke his hip, they reckon he's in hospital for a year!! | The real lesson is to keep stairs clear; the year in hospital is probably blown up | It proves only old people ever fall on stairs | The real lesson is to stop using stairs at home | Everything in it must be exactly true because it was so detailed
test: kid at school says his cousin slid down the banister and flew right out the window, swear down | It is most likely a tall tale; sliding on banisters is still a bad idea | It must be true, so banisters are safe if there is no window | It proves sliding is fine as long as you hold on | It means windows near stairs are the only danger
test: everyone's saying Mia cried on the stairs cos she fell in front of the whole class, shall i post it | No, keep it to yourself; it would only embarrass her | Yes, it is true so it is fine to post | Yes, but only to the people in your class | Yes, if you leave her name out but add a photo
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relative hazard of descending, as opposed to ascending, a staircase? | Descent is the more hazardous, since a loss of footing propels the body forward and downward | Ascent is the more hazardous, since the legs must raise the full weight of the body | The two are equally hazardous in every circumstance | Neither presents any hazard to a person of school age
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person traversing a staircase is advised to maintain contact with the handrail. | It affords a means of arresting the body should the person trip or slip | It reduces the wear sustained by the treads of the stairs over many years | It obliges the person to ascend at a greater speed | It prevents the staircase from moving under load
exam: It being given that a person must convey a load down a staircase, select the response that is consistent with established safe practice. | The load should be such that the steps and feet remain visible, one hand being kept free for the rail | The load should be held before the face so that it may be steadied with both hands | The load should be carried as rapidly as possible so as to shorten the descent | The load should be raised above the head with the arms fully extended
exam: Whereas socks are worn upon smooth wooden stairs, determine which measure most effectively diminishes the risk of slipping. | The removal of the socks or the wearing of shoes possessing grip | The pulling of the socks higher upon the leg | The descent of the stairs at a running pace | The carrying of a heavy object for added weight
